64:
“The taints by which one might be reborn in the deva realm, or become a garuḍa or a bird that flies through the sky;
The taints by which one might reach the state of a yakkha or go to the state of a human;
Those taints of mine are exhausted, destroyed, and completely eliminated.”

In this verse, [taints] refer to karma belonging to the three planes and the remaining unwholesome states. In the passage, “For the restraint of taints belonging to the present life, for the warding off of taints belonging to the future life,” [taints] refer to the various kinds of dangers that are the causes of suffering in the lower realms, such as the censure and accusations of others, remorse, killing, and binding. Those taints should be understood according to the manner in which they appear in whichever scriptural context they occur.

In the Vinaya, those taints first appear in two types: “For the restraint of taints belonging to the present life, for the warding off of taints belonging to the future life.” In the Saḷāyatana Saṃyutta, they appear in three types: “Friends, there are these three taints: the taint of sensual desire (kāmāsava), the taint of existence (bhavāsava), and the taint of ignorance (avijjāsava).” In other suttas and in the Abhidhamma, these same taints appear in four types along with the taint of views (diṭṭhāsava). In the Nibbedhika Sutta, they appear in five types: “Monks, there are taints leading to hell, taints leading to the animal realm, taints leading to the realm of ghosts (peta), taints leading to the human world, and taints leading to the deva world.” In the Chakka Nipāta, they appear in six types through the method beginning with: “Monks, there are taints to be abandoned by restraint.” In this sutta, however, these same taints appear in seven types along with the taints to be abandoned by seeing (the Path of Stream-entry). This is the etymological definition and the classification of the word "āsava."

Regarding the word "saṃvara" (restraint), it is called saṃvara because it restrains. It means it blocks, prevents, and does not allow [taints] to occur. Indeed, that meaning of blocking is correct. In such passages as: “Monks, I allow a monk who is resting during the day to rest after closing (saṃvaritvā) the door,” and “I tell you of the restraint of the streams; they are closed by wisdom,” the word saṃvara is used in the sense of closing or blocking. This restraint is of five types: restraint by virtue (sīla-saṃvara), restraint by mindfulness (sati-saṃvara), restraint by knowledge (ñāṇa-saṃvara), restraint by patience (khanti-saṃvara), and restraint by energy (vīriya-saṃvara). Among these five, in the passage, “He is endowed with this Pātimokkha restraint,” this restraint is called restraint by virtue.
